<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stuart Restoration</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1660">1660</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Restoration of 1660, English Restoration</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stuart Restoration was the return of the monarchy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and Ireland in 1660, when Charles II came back from exile after the collapse of the Commonwealth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>. In Scotland it revived the separate parliament, Privy Council and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, leading to conflict with the Presbyterian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenanters">Covenanters</a>. The term also names the period of Charles II's reign, known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and science.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stuart-restoration.jpg" alt="Stuart Restoration" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Michael Wright · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_II_of_England_in_Coronation_robes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Richard Cromwell, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-protector">Lord Protector</a> from 1658 to 1659, ceded power to the Rump Parliament, and for a year government was dominated by the army leaders Charles Fleetwood and John Lambert. On 20 October 1659 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-monck">George Monck</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> under the Cromwells, marched south to oppose them. Lambert's army melted away and Monck reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> unopposed. The Presbyterian members excluded in Pride's Purge of 1648 were recalled, and on 24 December the Long Parliament was restored.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 4 April 1660 Charles issued the Declaration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a>, setting out his promises on recovering the crown. Monck organised the Convention Parliament, which first met on 25 April and on 8 May proclaimed that Charles II had been lawful king since the execution of Charles I on 30 January 1649. Charles left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> on 23 May, landed at Dover on 25 May and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 29 May, his thirtieth birthday, a date kept afterwards as the holiday of Oak Apple Day. He was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> on 23 April 1661.</p><p>The Indemnity and Oblivion Act, law from 29 August 1660, pardoned past treason but excluded those involved in the trial and execution of Charles I. In October 1660 ten men were hanged, drawn and quartered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, among them Thomas Harrison and the preacher Hugh Peters; three more followed on 19 April 1662, and a further 19 were imprisoned for life. In January 1661 the corpses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>, Henry Ireton and John Bradshaw were exhumed and hanged in chains at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyburn">Tyburn</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Cavalier Parliament, overwhelmingly royalist, met on 8 May 1661 and sat until 24 January 1679. The leading minister of the first years was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-hyde-1st-earl-of-clarendon">Edward Hyde, 1st Earl of Clarendon</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> was restored as the national church, backed by the Clarendon Code and the Act of Uniformity 1662. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> the Rescissory Act 1661 annulled all legislation back to 1633; in Ireland the union of the Commonwealth years was treated as void, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-butler-1st-duke-of-ormonde">James Butler, 1st Duke of Ormond</a>, returned as Lord Lieutenant in 1662.</p><p>The colonies followed at different speeds. Barbados declared for the king in July 1660, Rhode Island in October 1660 and Massachusetts last, in August 1661; New Haven, which sheltered three regicides, was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> in 1662. The period is commonly taken to end with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1688, when William of Orange landed on 5 November and James II fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bill-of-rights-1689">Bill of Rights 1689</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stuart Restoration” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The prohibition ended in the year of the <b>Stuart Restoration</b>, and one carol crosses it: an early version of God Rest Ye Merry, Gentlemen is in an anonymous manuscript of the 1650s.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Commonwealth of England was restored briefly before the <b>Restoration of 1660</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/the-protectorate">The Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
